Z||000025||loafer87gt||11-12-2001||06:35 PM||||I'day our medical system could use a little improvement as well. Back in the good old days of the Conservative government we had a hospital in each town with well trained staff to operate them. Now after all the healthcare cutbacks the nearest "real" hospital is about a two hour drive away. I say this because they still kept the majority of the old hospitals open in the smaller communities but are now called health stations. They basically build false confidence in the communities, as the three health stations around my old home town are a farce. Last time I was back I cut hand fairly badly and none of the three health stations nearby had anyone capable of performing stitches on my hand. Another time I suffered heavy burns to my head and after driving checking in to all three health stations I was turned away by all of them, and sent to the nearest city. They were unable to provide even the most minor help such as gauze or salve to put on the burns to help the pain during the drive there. Finally, my mom has been having serious amnesia attacks where she forgets where she is, or who for that matter. She was supposed to have a MRI last October, but this has been postponed two times so far. Socialized health care is a great idea only if the government is willing to make a serious funding commitment to keep things running, but sadly the liberal government aren't in my opinion, are not willing to spend the money, and are not doing a good job of managing it.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Mach10||11-13-2001||03:00 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BN Boomer:<BR><B> I don't live very far from the border. (Yup, even get Canadian feeds, moron) My job also takes me up into Vancouver, BC. Your arguments obviously explain why so many doctors offices and medical clinics around here are clogged up with Canadians trying to get timely health care. And how financially sound is your health care? Oh, that's right, they just raise your taxes a little bit more to cover it. Why don't you just give your whole paycheck to your government so they can wipe your a$$ for the rest of your life? <P>I don't need or want some nanny government babysitting me from birth to grave. If you are such a lacky that is unable to take care of yourself and are totally dependent on such a sytem, be grateful for being born Canada.<P>You obviously do not understand or appreciate freedom. No one should have to explain to you or anyone why they want to own a gun. It's called freedom, you twit. Something that is obviously dwindling quickly in Canada. Maybe you should have to explain why you want to own something as dangerous as a vehicle that is capable of exceeding legal speed limits by such a large degree. Remember, cars still kill a lot more than guns. Yup, fast cars are a necessary part of life, ain't they? I'd suggest that you buy a clue, but with your poor economy and pathetic exchange rate, you probably can't afford one.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>In order:<P>Don't live far from the border, huh? Does the grass change color on the line between customs? I keep forgetting to check It does on my map...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>I live 45 minutes from the border... That don't make ME an expert on the States. That's why I won't comment on government practices in the states. All I know is what I see on TV. But I sees what I sees. <P>Good for you! You work in Vancouver occasionally! I got news for you. Visit any of our OTHER urban centers, and gawk at how different things are. Vancouver is not representative of how things are run up here. They vote WAY different, and they do things differently. It's a standing joke in Canada about how "Americanized" Vancouver is. My brother shattered a couple of ribs snow-boarding in Fernie. Was in the hospital waiting room for 30 minutes. Was in HOSPITAL for 3 days. <P>Don't know how it is there, but where I am, I have NEVER had to wait for attention. It's been like that EVERYWHERE I've had to get medical help. Maybe I'm just lucky, or maybe the claims aboot our troubled healthcare system are GROSSLY exaggerated by HMO proponents.<P>Financially sound?!? It's HEALTH CARE, not a business. Specific portion of the national budget/provincial budget is thrown at the hospitals. It's a BLACK HOLE. Unlike the HMO system... <P>Yeah, I pay taxes... You telling me that the "Land of the Free" Doesn't? Your taxes been stable since the 20s? Didn't think so. As a matter of fact, spending has been increased for Health/Education, *AND* my taxes are going down...<P>Don't want a Nanny government? Considering that I vote the bastards in every year, I'd want to get SOMETHING back.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>So you're telling me that if I was broke, and sick, and could not afford treatment, then it is my own damn fault for being poor? Uh... Ok... That's very nice of you.<P>I don't understand or appreciate freedom? That may very well be. Could be that I live in an oppressive society, and just don't know freedom when I see it? Maybe I'm harboring a deep jealousy for the States? Or maybe I just percieve a need for dangerous things being moderated... Cars are... It's called a license.... Isn't that what Gun Control is? Licensing it? Making the qualifications stricter so that the wrong people don't get ahold of it? I fail to see how that infringes on my freedom... I'm not arguing how dangerous guns are... I'm arguing that controlling who gets them is not impinging on my rights. There are people that SHOULD NOT DRIVE, and are kept off the roads... Similarly, there are people that should not own a gun... Again, where's thiss loss of freedom? Don't give me that conspiracy-theorist crap about how they take it a bit at a time...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"><P>Freedom is obviously dwindling in the Canada... You got us... From your Canadian Feeds, you MUST be seeing our children being taught to goose-step in school and I'm worried that I might get "silenced" for expressing my views here... Get over yourself man... How are our rights and freedoms being jeopardized? We have fairly strict gun-control, but *I* can still get a gun. No problems. Why do I need one again? Since you are such an expert on Canadian society and culture, (from your canadian feeds) you tell us. What can we NOT do now, that we could do 20 years ago? <P>I will agree on the exchange rate on clues. I can't afford ANY. Er, tell me again how this is relevant? <P>I am not stooping to name-calling. How about excercizing your right to carry on a CIVIL conversation? I'm willing to argue this back and forth for the next several months, but let's keep the name-calling in the school-yard, where it belongs<BR>||||reg||10
